<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmose, son of Ebana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amosis, Aahmes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmose, son of Ebana, was an Egyptian soldier, born in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elkab">Nekheb</a>, the modern El Kab, who served under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a>, Amenhotep I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a>. His father Baba had enlisted in the navy during the war to expel the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seqenenre-tao">Seqenenre Tao</a>. Ahmose fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>, the Hyksos capital in the Delta, where he killed two Hyksos and was twice awarded the gold of valor, took part in the three-year siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharuhen">Sharuhen</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a>. Under Thutmose I he was appointed admiral and followed the king against Naharin as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. The autobiography inscribed on the wall of his tomb is a valuable source for the late 17th and early 18th Dynasties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmose-son-of-ebana.jpg" alt="Ahmose, son of Ebana" width="330" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olaf Tausch; derivative work by JMCC1 ( talk ) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Necheb_Felsengrab_Ahmose_04_c.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
